<Project>
  <!-- Dependency advisories as a build gate, the NuGet spelling of the frontend job's
       `npm audit` at audit-level high (.github/workflows/ci.yml). (No double hyphens in this
       comment: XML forbids them, and MSBuild then refuses to load the file.)

       NuGetAuditMode=all audits transitive packages too, not only the ones a csproj names. That is
       where the first finding was: SSH.NET 2023.0.0, pulled in by Testcontainers 3.10.0, two high
       advisories (GHSA-mggc-4xg6-vcxf, GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284) that no csproj here mentions; it went
       with Testcontainers.PostgreSql 4.15.0 (SSH.NET 2026.0.0) in the change that added this gate.
       (SDK 10 already defaults to `all` for net10.0 projects; it is spelled out so the gate does not
       depend on the SDK's default.)

       WarningsAsErrors makes high (NU1903) and critical (NU1904) advisories fail restore, and with it
       `dotnet build`, `dotnet test` and package_lambda_zip.sh. Low (NU1901) and moderate (NU1902) are
       still printed as warnings, so they stay visible without turning CI red. Appended to, never
       replaced: the SDK adds its own entries to the same property.

       A props file, not the sibling Directory.Build.targets, because these are plain properties that
       restore reads; the targets file exists for item Removes, which need the SDK's items in place. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
